# ai-dev-switchboard configuration.
#
# Copy this file to switchboard.env (install.sh does this for you if it's
# missing) and fill in the values for your own setup. This file is a
# systemd EnvironmentFile — plain KEY=value, one per line, no quoting needed
# unless a value itself contains spaces.

# ── Who runs what ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RUN_USER: the unprivileged system user your coding sessions (Claude/aider/
# etc.) actually run as. Needs its own login, a home directory, and whatever
# credentials the engines you use need (e.g. `claude` logged in once
# interactively as this user). The switchboard's own process runs as a
# SEPARATE, more restricted user (see systemd/ai-dev-switchboard.service and
# scripts/install.sh) that can only run tmux/ttyd/code-server AS this user
# via a narrowly-scoped sudoers rule — never the reverse.
RUN_USER=dev

# Where project working copies live — one subdirectory per project, each
# one shows up as a toggleable row automatically. No registration step.
PROJECTS_DIR=/home/dev/projects

# ── Engines ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Directory of *.engine files (see engines.d/ in this repo for the format
# and shipped examples). Copy this whole directory to the target machine and
# point ENGINES_DIR at it — add/remove/edit *.engine files any time, no app
# restart required, they're read fresh on every session start.
ENGINES_DIR=/etc/ai-dev-switchboard/engines.d

# ── Auth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AUTH_MODE=pve   — log in with real Proxmox VE credentials (checked live
#                   against your PVE host's own API). Use this if the
#                   switchboard runs on/near a Proxmox host, like the
#                   original homelab setup this was built for.
# AUTH_MODE=simple — a single configured username/password instead. Use
#                   this if you have no Proxmox host at all.
AUTH_MODE=simple

# Required when AUTH_MODE=pve:
#PVE_HOST=192.168.178.100
#PVE_PORT=8006
#PVE_REALM=pam

# Required when AUTH_MODE=simple. SIMPLE_PASSWORD is a plain value in this
# file — protect switchboard.env's file permissions accordingly (install.sh
# sets it 0600, owned by the service user, by default).
#SIMPLE_USERNAME=admin
#SIMPLE_PASSWORD=change-me

# TOTP_SECRET: a base32 secret (RFC 6238) required either way. A code from
# it is asked for once per browser session, the moment you actually flip a
# switch — not bundled into login. install.sh generates a random one and
# prints it (as text and a QR code, if `qrencode` is available) if you don't
# set one here yourself. Add it to any authenticator app or a password
# manager's built-in TOTP field.
#TOTP_SECRET=

# ── Reaching the web UI and any per-session terminals it spawns ──────────
# PUBLISH_MODE and BASE_URL are asked interactively at install time
# (install.sh's "-- Publishing --" prompt, or ct/create.sh's equivalent
# menu on a Proxmox host) — you don't need to hand-edit this file just to
# set them. Edit them here (+ `systemctl restart ai-dev-switchboard`) only
# if you want to *change* the choice later.
# PUBLISH_MODE=tailscale — publish per-project ttyd/VS Code terminals at
#               BASE_URL/<path> via `tailscale serve --set-path` (requires
#               `tailscale` installed and logged in on this machine). This
#               is also how the main switchboard UI itself was reached in
#               the original setup: `tailscale serve` in front of
#               LISTEN_HOST:LISTEN_PORT, tailnet-only, no separate config
#               here for that part — see docs in README.md.
# PUBLISH_MODE=none — per-project terminals bind to 127.0.0.1 only and the
#               switchboard just reports that loopback URL; you're
#               responsible for exposing it yourself (SSH tunnel, your own
#               reverse proxy + auth, WireGuard, etc.) — same "the network
#               boundary IS the auth" model as tailscale mode, just BYO
#               boundary instead of Tailscale specifically.
PUBLISH_MODE=none

# Required when PUBLISH_MODE=tailscale — the tailnet hostname per-project
# terminals get mounted under, e.g. https://dev.your-tailnet.ts.net
#BASE_URL=

# What the switchboard's own web UI listens on. Put a reverse proxy /
# tailscale serve / SSH tunnel in front of this — it has no TLS of its own.
LISTEN_HOST=127.0.0.1
LISTEN_PORT=8333

# ── Optional: a persistent session on a separate "host" machine ─────────
# Beyond per-project sessions, the switchboard can also show one extra row
# that starts/stops a persistent session directly on another machine (e.g.
# the Proxmox host itself, outside any container) over a narrowly-scoped SSH
# key — see host-agent/README.md and docs/ARCHITECTURE.md. Off by default.
HOST_CONTROL_ENABLED=0

# Required when HOST_CONTROL_ENABLED=1:
#HOST_CONTROL_KEY=/home/switchboard-svc/.ssh/host_control_ed25519
#HOST_CONTROL_USER=switchboard
#HOST_IP=192.168.1.10
# Name the persistent host session gets started with (only matters for
# engines whose CMD uses {name}, e.g. Claude's --remote-control label).
#HOST_SESSION_NAME=host
# Which engine (a filename stem from ENGINES_DIR, without .engine) the host
# session uses. Independent from per-project engine choice.
#HOST_ENGINE=claude

# ── Optional: self-hosted Taiga (--with-taiga) ───────────────────────────
# A shared, singleton on/off toggle row for a self-hosted Taiga instance
# (project management / backlog tracker), run via its own official
# taiga-docker Compose stack — this project's first-ever Docker dependency.
# One instance for the whole box, not per switchboard project. Off by
# default even after `install.sh --with-taiga` — flip the toggle in the web
# UI to actually start it (9 containers, several GB RAM while running).
# install.sh sets all of these for you when you pass --with-taiga.
TAIGA_ENABLED=0

# Required when TAIGA_ENABLED=1:
#TAIGA_PORT=9000
#TAIGA_LABEL=Taiga
#TAIGA_UP_SCRIPT=/usr/local/bin/ai-dev-switchboard-taiga-up.sh
#TAIGA_DOWN_SCRIPT=/usr/local/bin/ai-dev-switchboard-taiga-down.sh
#TAIGA_STATUS_SCRIPT=/usr/local/bin/ai-dev-switchboard-taiga-status.sh

# TAIGA_DIR (the taiga-docker checkout path, e.g. /opt/ai-dev-switchboard-taiga)
# is also written here by install.sh, but only the three taiga-{up,down,status}.sh
# wrapper scripts read it — app.py itself never does, it only calls those
# scripts by their TAIGA_*_SCRIPT paths above.

# ── Optional: self-hosted Gitea (--with-git-hosting) ─────────────────────
# A shared, singleton on/off toggle row for a self-hosted Gitea instance
# (local git hosting), run via this project's own authored Docker Compose
# stack (server + db/Postgres) — folds into --with-git-hosting rather than
# a flag of its own (docs/spec.md backlog item 2a). One instance for the
# whole box, not per switchboard project. Off by default even after
# `install.sh --with-git-hosting` — flip the toggle in the web UI to
# actually start it (2 containers, well under 1 GB RAM while running — an
# order of magnitude lighter than Taiga's stack above). The web UI's
# "+ New project" button creates real Gitea repos via its own REST API
# (docs/spec.md backlog item 2b) once GITEA_API_TOKEN below is set — see
# docs/GIT_HOSTING.md. install.sh sets all of these for you when you pass
# --with-git-hosting.
GITEA_ENABLED=0

# Required when GITEA_ENABLED=1:
#GITEA_PORT=3000
#GITEA_LABEL=Gitea
#GITEA_UP_SCRIPT=/usr/local/bin/ai-dev-switchboard-gitea-up.sh
#GITEA_DOWN_SCRIPT=/usr/local/bin/ai-dev-switchboard-gitea-down.sh
#GITEA_STATUS_SCRIPT=/usr/local/bin/ai-dev-switchboard-gitea-status.sh
#NEW_PROJECT_FROM_GITEA_SCRIPT=/usr/local/bin/ai-dev-switchboard-new-project-from-gitea.sh

# GITEA_API_TOKEN: a Gitea Personal Access Token (scope
# write:repository,write:user),
# required for "+ New project" to actually work. NOT set by install.sh --
# minted by a separate one-time step, scripts/gitea-configure-api.sh, run
# once (as root) after both Gitea is toggled on AND its own admin account
# already exists (see docs/GIT_HOSTING.md). Left commented here the same way
# every other secret this file documents-but-never-ships-a-value-for is
# (SIMPLE_PASSWORD, TOTP_SECRET) — until it's set, "+ New project" returns a
# clear message pointing back at gitea-configure-api.sh instead of a raw
# failure.
#GITEA_API_TOKEN=

# GITEA_DIR (e.g. /opt/ai-dev-switchboard-gitea) and GITEA_SSH_PORT (default
# 2222, loopback-only — the web UI never touches it) are also written here by
# install.sh, but only the three gitea-{up,down,status}.sh wrapper scripts
# read them — app.py itself never does, it only calls those scripts by their
# GITEA_*_SCRIPT paths above (and NEW_PROJECT_FROM_GITEA_SCRIPT for repo
# creation).

# Poll-based sync-on-push (backlog item 2c, part 1 — docs/spec.md,
# docs/GIT_HOSTING.md). Keeps PROJECTS_DIR/<name> in sync when a push lands
# on its Gitea repo from somewhere else (another contributor via Gitea's own
# web UI, a merged PR, a second agent session elsewhere) — no webhook, no new
# listener, no new secret: app.py periodically asks Gitea's own REST API
# (already reachable over GITEA_PORT above) whether a project's default
# branch has moved, and only then fetches + fast-forwards (never
# `git reset --hard`) via GITEA_SYNC_SCRIPT, run as RUN_USER (not root).
# install.sh sets both of these for you when you pass --with-git-hosting.
#GITEA_SYNC_SCRIPT=/usr/local/bin/ai-dev-switchboard-gitea-sync-project.sh
#GITEA_REPO_MAP_FILE=/var/lib/ai-dev-switchboard/gitea-repo-map.json

# How often the poll pass itself runs (seconds) — independent of the web
# UI's own fast 4-second /status poll, since this is a real network call per
# Gitea-backed project, not free in-memory bookkeeping. NOT written by
# install.sh (an arbitrary-but-reasonable built-in default lives in app.py
# itself) — uncomment to override. Sync latency after a real push is bounded
# by this value (up to ~45s by default), never instant.
#GITEA_POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS=45

# ── Optional: switchboard-side deploy dispatch (backlog item 2c, part 2b) ─
# A per-project "Deploy" button that pushes PROJECTS_DIR/<name> to a
# pre-provisioned deploy-target/ receiver (see deploy-target/README.md,
# install.sh --with-deploy-target, run on a *separate* target machine) and
# triggers its restart — manual only, never automatic off a Gitea push (see
# docs/spec.md's part 2c part 2b "Deliberate deviation from
# docs/BACKLOG.md"). Independent of GITEA_ENABLED: a project doesn't need
# Gitea at all to have a deploy target.
#
# DEPLOY_MAP_FILE is a hand-edited, operator-maintained project -> target
# map (host/port/user/deploy_path/service/key per project — see
# config/deploy-map.json.example for the exact schema). app.py only ever
# reads this file, never writes it. install.sh creates a safe, empty
# ({}) default here the first time it's missing, and never touches it again
# on re-run — hand-edit it yourself to actually add projects.
#DEPLOY_MAP_FILE=/etc/ai-dev-switchboard/deploy-map.json

# DEPLOY_KEYS_DIR is the mode-700, SVC_USER-owned directory every map
# entry's "key" (an SSH private key you generate and place there yourself,
# same precedent as deploy-target/README.md's own keypair-placement step)
# must resolve under — a hand-edited map entry pointing anywhere else is
# silently dropped (treated the same as "no entry"), not a crash.
# install.sh creates this directory (permissions/ownership only, never
# key content) unconditionally, every run.
#DEPLOY_KEYS_DIR=/etc/ai-dev-switchboard/deploy-keys

# ── Optional: per-project one-line descriptions via a local LLM ─────────
# If set, a background thread summarizes each project's README/CLAUDE.md/
# AGENTS.md/package.json once (cached to disk) using this OpenAI-compatible
# chat-completions endpoint. Left unset, the description feature is simply
# off — no other functionality depends on it.
#DESC_LLM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1
#DESC_LLM_MODEL=qwen3:8b

# ── Folder-upload wizard (works standalone — no --with-git-hosting needed) ─
# Lets you register a project by picking a local folder or an already-made
# .zip through the web UI instead of a shell (see docs/spec.md "Folder
# upload -> auto-detect repo(s)"). install.sh installs and configures all of
# this for you unconditionally, unlike the git-hosting "+ New project" flow.

# Where uploaded zips are staged (unprivileged — owned by the switchboard's
# own service user) between phase 1 (upload/detect) and phase 2 (confirm/
# register). Created automatically at startup if missing.
UPLOAD_STAGING_DIR=/var/lib/ai-dev-switchboard/uploads

# Largest accepted upload, checked twice — once against Content-Length
# before reading any body bytes, once against the opened zip's own
# uncompressed total (catches a zip-bomb-shaped mismatch). See docs/spec.md
# "Size limits".
UPLOAD_MAX_BYTES=104857600

# How long an uploaded-but-never-confirmed staging directory is kept before
# being swept as abandoned (docs/spec.md "Two-phase protocol"). A confirmed
# upload's staging directory is removed immediately regardless of this.
UPLOAD_STAGING_TTL_SECONDS=1800

# The privileged hand-off script that actually moves a validated, staged
# directory into PROJECTS_DIR/<name> as RUN_USER — see
# scripts/new-project-from-upload.sh and docs/ARCHITECTURE.md's
# privilege-boundary section. install.sh installs and sets this for you.
NEW_PROJECT_FROM_UPLOAD_SCRIPT=/usr/local/bin/ai-dev-switchboard-new-project-from-upload.sh

# Cheap guard against a many-tiny-files zip (a DoS shape) — rejects an
# upload whose zip has more than this many entries. Uncomment to override.
#UPLOAD_MAX_ENTRIES=20000

# ── Optional: headless engine invocation (backlog item 6a) ──────────────
# app/teams.py's agent_run() -- one bounded, non-interactive engine turn,
# spawned inside a throwaway tmux session (the same TMUX sudoers rule
# above, zero new privilege surface) -- see docs/spec.md and
# docs/ADDING_AN_ENGINE.md's HEADLESS_* section. Not wired into app.py's own
# HTTP handler yet (that's 6d); today this is a library function plus
# `python3 app/teams.py run/list-engines`, nothing user-facing.

# Where both the durable per-run .jsonl event logs and each run's throwaway
# state directory (TEAM_STATE_DIR/_headless/<run_id>/) live. Created
# automatically if missing, same as UPLOAD_STAGING_DIR above.
#TEAM_STATE_DIR=/var/lib/ai-dev-switchboard/teams

# How long a headless run is allowed before agent_run() sends a targeted
# SIGTERM to the engine process (never the tmux session, never capture-pane)
# -- escalates to SIGKILL, then a last-resort tmux kill-session, if the
# process doesn't exit within TEAM_HEADLESS_KILL_GRACE_SECONDS of each step.
#TEAM_HEADLESS_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=600
#TEAM_HEADLESS_KILL_GRACE_SECONDS=10

# How often agent_run() polls for new stream output / a written exit code.
# Trades latency against overhead -- fine for one run at a time; may want
# tuning once 6c's lead loop or 6f's overwatch feed have many runs in flight
# at once (see docs/spec.md "Open questions").
#TEAM_HEADLESS_POLL_SECONDS=0.5

# Stream-volume caps -- stop further per-line event translation (with one
# truncation-notice event) without affecting the real exit code/ok result.
#TEAM_HEADLESS_MAX_EVENTS=2000
#TEAM_HEADLESS_MAX_LINE_BYTES=1048576

# Prompt byte caps -- two, not one, since HEADLESS_PROMPT=arg has a much
# tighter ceiling (the prompt ends up as its own argv element when the
# engine binary is exec'd, capped by Linux's per-argv-element
# MAX_ARG_STRLEN) than stdin/file delivery does, which has no such limit --
# the prompt never appears in any argv at all in those modes.
#TEAM_HEADLESS_ARG_PROMPT_MAX_BYTES=65536
#TEAM_HEADLESS_PROMPT_MAX_BYTES=1048576

# How much of a run's stderr is kept (tail end) in the returned result's
# stderr_tail, e.g. to explain a nonzero exit code.
#TEAM_HEADLESS_STDERR_TAIL_BYTES=4096

# A run directory older than this, whose tmux session no longer exists
# either, is swept opportunistically at the start of the next agent_run()
# call -- covers app.py's/teams.py's own process being restarted mid-run.
#TEAM_HEADLESS_STALE_RUN_TTL_SECONDS=7200

# ── Optional: team grounding -- discovery, digest, fact_check (6b) ──────
# app/teams.py's load_grounding()/build_digest()/fact_check() -- auto-
# discovers a project's own docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, docs/BACKLOG.md,
# CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md, README.md and builds a bounded summary for a future
# team lead's system prompt (docs/spec.md, docs/story.md §4.3). Read-only;
# not wired into any HTTP route or UI yet (6c/6d). Measures the assembled
# digest string's own UTF-8 byte length -- not any source file's size, and
# not the (much larger, fixed, non-configurable) 2 MiB single-file read cap.
#TEAM_GROUNDING_MAX_BYTES=8000

# ── Optional: team lead loop (6c) ────────────────────────────────────────
# app/teams.py's roster()/team_run() -- the four-tool lead loop (delegate/
# fact_check/ask_user/finish) driving 6a's agent_run() and 6b/6b.1's
# grounding into an actual team. CLI-only (python3 app/teams.py roster |
# team-start | team-status | team-resolve | team-resume) -- no web route,
# no UI yet (6d/6e/6f).

# TEAM_LLM_BASE_URL/TEAM_LLM_MODEL point at a real OpenAI-compatible
# /v1/chat/completions endpoint (an Ollama install, local or remote) used
# ONLY for the tier-1 (native tool-calling) lead adapter -- deliberately a
# SIBLING of, not shared with, DESC_LLM_BASE_URL/DESC_LLM_MODEL above (a
# model good at a one-line project description is not necessarily a good
# tool-caller). Unset means the roster simply has no tier-1 member; an
# engines.d-based lead (tier 2/3) still works with zero Ollama config at
# all. The standard container this project targets has ~715MB free RAM
# with swap exhausted -- no tool-capable model fits locally, so this is
# expected to point at a REMOTE endpoint (docs/story.md §2.5), the same way
# install.sh --with-ollama links one rather than installing one.
#TEAM_LLM_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1
#TEAM_LLM_MODEL=qwen3:8b

# Per-HTTP-call timeout to the tier-1 endpoint. The 6c spike measured mean
# 7.4s, max 20.8s over 10 calls against an idle remote Ollama; 120 is ~6x
# the observed max, not the max itself, deliberately generous rather than
# tight.
#TEAM_LLM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=120

# Retries for a TRANSPORT-layer failure only (URLError/timeout/HTTP 5xx, or
# a response body that isn't parseable JSON at all) talking to the tier-1
# endpoint -- never a model-output-quality problem. 2 means 3 attempts
# total before team_step() surfaces a clear, actionable, non-traceback
# operational error (status="error").
#TEAM_LLM_TRANSPORT_RETRY_BUDGET=2

# Hard round ceiling across the whole lead loop, all tiers. Reaching this
# without finish/ask_user forces a terminal "escalated_max_rounds" status
# rather than looping forever.
#TEAM_MAX_ROUNDS=8

# Retries for a lead action that CANNOT be turned into a valid tool call at
# all, any tier -- unknown tool name, missing/wrong-typed required args,
# unparsable tier-2/tier-3 JSON. 2 means 3 attempts total before the loop
# forces an ask_user escalation with the raw unparsable text included. One
# shared budget across all three tiers (an unreachable-endpoint problem and
# a garbled-output problem are different failure classes with different
# correct responses, but "garbled output" itself means the same thing
# regardless of which tier produced it).
#TEAM_LEAD_MALFORMED_RETRY_BUDGET=2

# Cap on the MOST RECENT delegation's raw agent_run() result text, folded
# verbatim into the next round's prompt (older rounds only ever get a
# one-line summary, never their own full text). ~1000 tokens by default.
#TEAM_DELEGATE_RESULT_MAX_CHARS=4000

# FINAL, unconditional cap on the entire assembled per-round prompt (system
# framing + grounding digest + round history + most-recent capped result),
# applied last regardless of whether every smaller budget above it was
# individually respected -- the same "the heuristic above it doesn't have
# to be right, because the final step always runs" pattern build_digest()
# already uses for TEAM_GROUNDING_MAX_BYTES.
#TEAM_LEAD_PROMPT_MAX_CHARS=20000

# ── Optional: team session lifecycle -- worktrees + tmux dashboard (6d pt 1) ─
# app/teams.py's launch_team()/stop_team()/sweep_dead_teams() -- one git
# worktree per teammate under PROJECTS_DIR/<name>.teams/<agent>/, a
# persistent team-<project> tmux session with one dashboard window per agent
# (a `tail -F` over that agent's own stable, append-mode event log -- not a
# window whose command is the headless CLI itself). CLI-only (python3
# app/teams.py team-launch | team-stop | team-reap) -- no web route, no
# background thread yet (6d part 2). Every RUN_USER-owned filesystem/tmux
# operation this adds goes through the SAME TMUX sudoers rule above -- no
# new privileged path.

# Bounds a single `git worktree add`/`remove` invocation (via
# _run_run_user_command()). 30s is generous for a well-behaved git command
# against a local filesystem -- a hung git process (e.g. an index lock held
# by another process) is the only realistic way to hit this.
#TEAM_WORKTREE_OP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# TTL backstop for a team-<project> tmux session/its worktrees once the run
# has reached a terminal status (finished/escalated_max_rounds/error/
# stopped) -- mirrors UPLOAD_STAGING_TTL_SECONDS's own precedent: a resource
# deliberately NOT torn down in a `finally`, reclaimed opportunistically
# instead by sweep_dead_teams() (called at the top of team-launch/team-stop,
# plus its own team-reap subcommand). NEVER applies to a run sitting
# status=="blocked_ask_user", at any age -- a human may still be about to
# answer it, and there's no operational signal yet for "how long is
# abandoned" (docs/spec.md "Open questions").
#TEAM_SESSION_STALE_TTL_SECONDS=86400

# ── Binaries (defaults match install.sh's install locations) ────────────
TTYD_BIN=/usr/local/bin/ttyd
CODE_SERVER_BIN=/usr/local/bin/code-server
